# Patterns excluded from `helm package`. Matched with Go filepath.Match against every path in the
# chart directory, so a bare name matches at any depth and a leading `/` anchors it to the root.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS ACTUALLY FOR HERE. Almost every chart's .helmignore is boilerplate, because
# almost every chart directory holds nothing but templates. This one holds files/ -- 17 directories
# of repository-owned configuration mirrored in by scripts/sync-helm-chart-files.mjs, because Helm
# cannot read outside its own chart. That mirror is a copy of things that live elsewhere in the
# repository, so anything an editor, an OS or a tool drops next to the originals gets copied in
# beside them and then packaged into a release artefact. Without this file, `helm package` says
# nothing about any of it.

# --- version control and editors -------------------------------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*.bak
*~

# --- OS litter -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .DS_Store and Thumbs.db are the two that reliably reach a repository from a shared checkout.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- build output and dependencies -------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.tgz

# --- packaging artefacts -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A previously packaged chart sitting in the chart directory would otherwise be packaged INTO the
# next one. Anchored, because a legitimately-named directory deeper in files/ should not be caught.
/*.tgz
/dist/

# --- the two values files, and the argument that was made both ways ----------------------------
# THEY ARE IGNORED. They are passed by path (-f) and never read from inside the chart, so the only
# thing carrying them would be the archive Helm stores with every release -- which shares a 1 MiB
# Secret with the rendered manifest.
#
# The case against was that a published chart has no other way to carry them:
# values-prod.yaml.example is the only place the optional hardening features are turned on together
# with the reasoning for each, and `helm show values` renders values.yaml alone. That case stands
# for someone who pulled the chart from a registry with no repository beside it -- and it loses to
# the ceiling, because a chart that cannot be upgraded documents nothing. docs/install.md and
# deploy/k8s/README.md both name them in the repository.
#
# This paragraph used to argue the opposite immediately above the two lines that ignore them, so
# the file could not say which was true. It is the ignore.
values-dev.yaml
values-prod.yaml.example
# Written by npm run setup and npm run try, holding a site's credentials. Never packaged.
values-local.yaml
values-try.yaml
